          Pokémon GO is a location game, and a treadmill is the one kind of walking that leaves your
          location exactly where it started. That is why the game, left open on the console, seems
          to ignore a forty-minute walk. The workaround is a feature the game already has:
          Adventure Sync, which credits distance from Apple Health or Google Fit even when the app is
          closed. Used properly, it can turn treadmill walks into hatched eggs and buddy candy. Used
          carelessly, it counts nothing — and the shortcut some players reach for instead,
          location spoofing, breaks the game’s rules and can cost you the account.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Does Pokémon GO Work on a Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Yes, but only through Adventure Sync.</strong> With the app open, Pokémon GO
            follows you on its map, and on a treadmill you never move on the map, so an open game
            does not usefully count treadmill walking. Adventure Sync is different: the Pokémon GO
            Help Center describes it as an optional mode that records kilometres travelled even when
            the app is not open, using data from Apple Health on iPhone or Google Fit on Android.
            Steps your phone counts on the treadmill go into that health app, and Adventure Sync can
            then credit the distance toward eggs and buddy candy.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three conditions matter. Your phone has to be on your body, not in the console tray,
            because a phone that does not move does not count steps. Adventure Sync has to be
            switched on, with location permission set to always allow. And you need patience: the
            help centre warns that syncing with Apple Health or Google Fit can take up to several
            hours.
          </p>
          <p>
            What does not work, and what we do not recommend, is faking your location. The game’s
            Gameplay Fairness Policy names GPS location spoofing as cheating, under a three-strike
            policy that ends in a permanent ban. If your step counts look off, our guide to whether
            <a href="/does-a-fitness-tracker-count-steps-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">a fitness tracker counts steps on a treadmill</a>
            covers the measurement side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-adventure-sync-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How Adventure Sync Counts Distance</h2>
<p>
            Adventure Sync was introduced in 2018 so that players could earn credit for walking
            without keeping the game open. The help centre describes what it does now:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>It records the distance you travel while the Pokémon GO app is closed, so you can
            hatch eggs and earn buddy candy without the game on screen.</li>
            <li>It connects to Apple Health on Apple devices or Google Fit on Android, and needs
            permission to read that data.</li>
            <li>It requires location permission set to always allow, even when the app is closed.</li>
            <li>It is available from Trainer Level 5 upwards.</li>
            <li>It adds weekly fitness reports and rewards for reaching weekly goals, and an optional
            home-screen widget that can take up to an hour to show new distance.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Why that helps on a treadmill.</strong> Apple Health and Google Fit can estimate
            walking distance from the phone’s motion sensors, not just from GPS. On a treadmill, the
            phone in your pocket registers your steps, the health app turns them into distance, and
            Adventure Sync reads that distance later. Your position on the game map never changes, but
            the step-based distance still accumulates.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Checking what it has counted.</strong> The help centre says you can view your
            current Adventure Sync stats from your Trainer Profile, and the weekly report summarises
            the week’s activity. Those are the numbers to compare against your health app when you
            are working out whether a treadmill session was credited. Check the same day in both, and
            allow for the sync delay before deciding something is missing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it will not count.</strong> The troubleshooting article is explicit that
            fitness data entered manually into Apple Health or Google Fit does not count toward
            Adventure Sync. Typing in your treadmill console distance afterwards will not work.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Android and Health Connect.</strong> On 17 October 2026 the Pokémon GO help pages
            still named Google Fit as the Android source and did not mention Health Connect. The help
            centre also says that if your Android device is incompatible with Google Fit, you cannot
            use Adventure Sync’s fitness tracking. If your phone offers a different health platform
            when you switch Adventure Sync on, follow the in-game prompt and the current help
            centre, not older guides.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How to Turn On Adventure Sync for Treadmill Walking</h2>
<p>
            Set this up before your walk, not during it. The steps in the Pokémon GO Help Center are:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>In Pokémon GO, tap the Main Menu, then Settings.</li>
            <li>Tap General, then Adventure Sync, and switch it on.</li>
            <li>When prompted, allow Pokémon GO to access your Apple Health or Google Fit data.</li>
            <li>Set the app’s location permission to always allow. On iPhone, the help centre gives
            the route as Settings, Privacy, Location Services, then the app, set to Always. On
            Android it is the app’s permissions in your device settings, and the exact path varies
            by phone.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Then check the health app side, which is where most problems start:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Apple Health:</strong> open Health, go to Sources, and make sure Pokémon GO is
            listed as a connected app.</li>
            <li><strong>Google Fit:</strong> in its settings, under managing connected applications,
            make sure Pokémon GO is listed.</li>
            <li><strong>Battery saving:</strong> the help centre says your phone’s battery-saving mode
            can disable the sensors Adventure Sync needs, and that battery-saving options inside
            Google Fit or Apple Health can interfere too. Switch them off.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Before trusting it with a long treadmill session, do a short test: walk for ten minutes
            with the phone in your pocket, then check the distance in Apple Health or Google Fit. If
            the health app shows the walk, Adventure Sync has something to read.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="phone-placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Where to Keep Your Phone on a Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            This single decision explains most "Adventure Sync does not work on my treadmill"
            complaints. A phone counts steps from its own movement. On a treadmill, the console
            tray, cup holder and tablet shelf are the places your phone moves least.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Best: a snug pocket or running belt.</strong> A trouser pocket, a zipped
            leggings pocket or a waist belt keeps the phone moving with your hips, which is close to
            how it counts steps on an outdoor walk.</li>
            <li><strong>Workable: an armband.</strong> It moves with your arm swing rather than your
            stride, so the count may differ from a pocket, but it does move.</li>
            <li><strong>Poor: the console or tablet holder.</strong> The phone sits still while the
            belt moves underneath you, so it records little or nothing. A loose jacket pocket that
            swings can over-count.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Holding the handrails changes the picture as well. With your hands on the rails, a phone
            in your pocket still moves with your hips, but a watch on your wrist does not swing, and
            your walking pattern changes. Our guide on whether you
            <a href="/should-you-hold-the-handrails-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">should hold the handrails on a treadmill</a>
            explains why letting go, once you are steady, is better for the workout too.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you want a rough check, compare the steps your phone records with a known figure. Our
            guide to
            <a href="/how-many-steps-in-a-mile-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how many steps are in a mile on a treadmill</a>
            shows how to work out your own steps per mile.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="watch-steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Do Steps From an Apple Watch or Other Watch Count?</h2>
<p>
            This is the question we could not settle from official sources, so here is exactly what
            is and is not known.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the help centre says.</strong> Pokémon GO ended support for connecting an
            Apple Watch directly on 1 July 2019. The help article explaining that change says
            players can track distance through Adventure Sync by syncing the fitness apps on their
            phones, which does not require devices such as Apple Watches. Separately, the
            troubleshooting article says your phone must have the sensors to track distance and
            steps, and that manually entered data does not count.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What it does not say.</strong> None of the help pages we checked says whether
            distance a watch writes into Apple Health or Google Fit is counted by Adventure Sync, or
            ignored. Fan guides describe watches and other trackers being picked up, but they are
            not official and some are several years old.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The practical approach.</strong> Keep the phone on your body even if you are
            wearing a watch, because the phone is the source Pokémon GO documents. If you want to know
            whether your watch adds anything, compare the Adventure Sync weekly distance in your
            Trainer Profile with the distance your health app shows for the same days. If you are
            wearing a watch mainly for the workout itself, our guide on
            <a href="/how-to-connect-apple-watch-to-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">connecting an Apple Watch to a treadmill</a>
            covers getting accurate treadmill data from it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Does Speed Matter? Walking vs Running on the Belt</h2>
<p>
            Players often ask whether there is a speed limit on Adventure Sync, and whether running
            on a treadmill counts as well as walking. The honest answer is that the official help
            pages do not say.
          </p>
          <p>
            The game has long been designed to discourage counting distance travelled by car, and fan
            sites quote various speed thresholds. Those figures disagree with one another, and none
            of the Pokémon GO Help Center articles we checked publishes one for Adventure Sync, so we
            do not repeat them here. The help pages describe Adventure Sync in terms of distance
            travelled and steps, and the egg article simply says eggs hatch as you walk.
          </p>
          <p>
            What that means in practice:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walking at a normal pace is the use the game describes.</strong> If hatching
            eggs is the goal, an ordinary brisk walk is the safest bet. Our guide to
            <a href="/what-speed-should-i-walk-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what speed to walk on a treadmill</a>
            helps you pick one.</li>
            <li><strong>If you run, measure the result.</strong> After a treadmill run, compare the
            distance in Apple Health or Google Fit with what Adventure Sync credits over the next few
            hours. That tells you more about your set-up than any figure quoted online.</li>
            <li><strong>Phone distance and console distance will differ.</strong> The phone
            estimates distance from steps; the treadmill calculates it from belt speed. Neither is
            what Pokémon GO reads directly — it reads the health app.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="hatching-eggs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Can You Hatch Eggs on a Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, through Adventure Sync. The help centre’s egg article explains that each egg shows
            the number of kilometres you must walk for it to hatch, that you place it in an
            incubator, and that with Adventure Sync on, your kilometres walked count even while the
            app is closed. With Adventure Sync off, you need to keep the app open while you walk —
            which, on a treadmill, is the situation where the game sees you standing still.
          </p>
          <p>
            A sensible treadmill egg routine:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>Before you start, open the game and put eggs in every incubator you have. The help
            centre notes that each incubator holds one egg and that you start with one infinite-use
            incubator; extra incubators can be bought in the in-game shop.</li>
            <li>Check Adventure Sync is on and the phone is not in battery-saving mode.</li>
            <li>Close the game, put the phone in a pocket or belt, and walk.</li>
            <li>Afterwards, give it time. The help centre warns of a delay of up to several hours
            for the game to sync with Apple Health or Google Fit, and suggests restarting the app
            may help.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The distances on eggs are in kilometres, while most US treadmills display miles. One mile
            is a little over 1.6 kilometres. The table below converts some round distances into miles
            and into walking time at two common treadmill speeds, by the console. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-is-a-mile-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long a mile takes on a treadmill</a>
            converts other speeds if you walk faster or slower.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Distance</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">In miles</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">At 3.0 mph</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">At 3.5 mph</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 km</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">0.62 mi</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 12 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 11 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 km</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.24 mi</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 25 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 21 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 km</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1.86 mi</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 37 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 32 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 km</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.11 mi</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 62 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 53 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7 km</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4.35 mi</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 87 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 75 min</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 km</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6.21 mi</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 124 min</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">about 107 min</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Those times are simple arithmetic from the console speed. Your phone estimates distance
            from your steps, not from the belt, so the distance Adventure Sync credits can come out a
            little higher or lower than the console shows. If it is consistently short, walk a few
            minutes longer rather than chasing the exact figure.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Daily Adventure Egg</h3>
          <p>
            The help centre describes a Daily Adventure Egg for Trainers at Level 15 and above. When
            you first log in on a new day, it is placed automatically in its own incubator, so it
            does not use one of yours or count toward your egg storage, and it hatches after 1 km of
            exploring. You can only hold one at a time, and a new one arrives after midnight local
            time. The help centre also says it is not eligible for event hatch bonuses. For a
            treadmill walker, it is the easiest daily target: open the game before your walk so the
            egg is placed, then close it and walk a little over half a mile by the console.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you play another of the same company’s games with Adventure Sync, such as Pikmin
            Bloom, the help centre says distance is shared across them when your accounts use the same
            login, and you get credit for the highest distance reported rather than a double count.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-not-counting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Why Your Treadmill Distance Is Not Counting</h2>
<p>
            Work through these in order. Most fixes take a minute.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Is the walk in your health app?</strong> Open Apple Health or Google Fit. If
            the treadmill walk is missing there, the problem is phone placement or sensors, not
            Pokémon GO.</li>
            <li><strong>Was the phone moving?</strong> A phone on the console records little. Move
            it to a pocket or belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Is Adventure Sync actually on?</strong> Check that it is ticked in Settings,
            General, Adventure Sync.</li>
            <li><strong>Is location set to always allow?</strong> The help centre says iOS may ask
            you to confirm this more than once, and that Adventure Sync is switched off unless you
            choose always allow.</li>
            <li><strong>Is battery saving on?</strong> On the phone or inside the health app, it can
            stop the sensors Adventure Sync relies on.</li>
            <li><strong>Did you type the distance in?</strong> Manually entered fitness data does
            not count.</li>
            <li><strong>Have you waited?</strong> Syncing can take up to several hours. Restart the
            app and check again later.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If the health app shows the walk, Adventure Sync is on, and it still does not count after
            several hours, the help centre suggests contacting support through the app.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When it counts, but less than the console</h3>
          <p>
            This is the most common partial result, and it is usually not a fault. The treadmill
            calculates distance from belt speed; the phone estimates it from the number of steps it
            detects and an assumed stride length. Short, quick steps on a belt, a phone that shifts in
            a loose pocket, or a stride that differs from the phone’s assumption all move the figure.
            The health app’s number is the one Adventure Sync reads, so that is the one to compare
            with what the game credits. If the console itself might be off, our guide to whether
            <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">your treadmill speed is accurate</a>
            shows how to check the belt with a tape measure and a timer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="spoofing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Why Not Just Spoof Your Location?</h2>
<p>
            Search for this topic and you will find apps and gadgets that claim to make the game think
            you are walking. We do not recommend any of them, and we will not explain how they work,
            because using them breaks the game’s rules.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Pokémon GO Help Center’s Gameplay Fairness Policy defines cheating as behaviour that
            violates the Terms of Service, the Player Guidelines or the Live Event Code of Conduct,
            and names falsifying location — GPS location spoofing — and accessing the game through
            unauthorised third-party software or add-ons as examples. The Player Guidelines list
            using tools or techniques to alter or falsify your location as cheating, alongside
            modified or unofficial software.
          </p>
          <p>
            The consequences are set out plainly:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Strike 1, a warning:</strong> roughly seven days of degraded gameplay, such as
            losing rare encounters, trading and gifting.</li>
            <li><strong>Strike 2, a suspension:</strong> roughly 30 days locked out of the account.</li>
            <li><strong>Strike 3, termination:</strong> a permanent ban.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The policy also says offenders are not always offered three strikes, and some behaviour
            can lead to immediate termination without warning. It adds that very few terminations
            are overturned on appeal. Years of progress are a lot to risk to save a walk you were
            going to do anyway. Adventure Sync exists precisely so you do not have to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Playing Safely on a Moving Belt</h2>
<p>
            Pokémon GO is designed to be played while moving, but a treadmill is not a pavement.
            Looking down at a phone on a moving belt shifts your balance and your attention, and the
            belt keeps going whether you are watching it or not.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Walk first, play after.</strong> With Adventure Sync, there is no reason to
            have the game on screen during the walk. Close it, pocket the phone and look ahead.</li>
            <li><strong>If you want the screen, stop the belt.</strong> Catching, battling or raiding
            while walking is a recipe for drifting to the edge of the deck. Step onto the side rails
            or pause the treadmill first.</li>
            <li><strong>Clip on the safety key.</strong> It stops the belt if you drift back too far.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep children and pets away.</strong> A game that is fun to watch over
            someone’s shoulder draws an audience close to the rear roller.</li>
            <li><strong>Mind the headphones and cables.</strong> A charging cable running from the
            console to a pocket can snag; charge the phone before the walk instead.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            None of this is a reason to avoid playing. It is a reason to separate the walking from
            the playing, which Adventure Sync makes easy: the walk earns the distance, and the game
            can wait until you have stepped off.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety guide</a>
            covers the rest of the household rules.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="walking-pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Walking Pads and Desk Walking for Adventure Sync</h2>
<p>
            Walking pads and under-desk treadmills are, in a sense, ideal for Adventure Sync: slow,
            steady walking for long periods while you do something else. The same rules apply. The
            phone needs to be on your body — in a pocket rather than on the desk — and the walk has to
            show up in Apple Health or Google Fit before the game can credit it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Desk walking has one particular catch. If you type while you walk, your arms stay still,
            so a wrist tracker may count far fewer steps than you take. A phone in a trouser pocket
            moves with your hips and is less affected. Our
            <a href="/best-fitness-trackers-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">fitness trackers for walking pads</a>
            guide covers devices that cope better.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you are thinking of buying a pad partly to rack up walking distance, our
            <a href="/walking-pad-buying-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad buying guide</a>
            covers belt length, noise and the quality differences between budget brands, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-walking-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill walking workouts</a>
            give you sessions that are more interesting than a single steady pace.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Pokémon GO work on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, through Adventure Sync. With the app open, the game tracks your position on its map, which does not change on a treadmill. Adventure Sync instead reads distance from Apple Health or Google Fit, even with the app closed, so steps your phone counts on the treadmill can go toward eggs and buddy candy. Keep the phone in a pocket, not on the console.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Adventure Sync work on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. Adventure Sync credits distance recorded by Apple Health on iPhone or Google Fit on Android, and those apps can estimate walking distance from the phone's motion sensors. Switch Adventure Sync on, set location to always allow, turn off battery saving, and keep the phone on your body. Syncing can take up to several hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you hatch eggs on a treadmill in Pokémon GO?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with Adventure Sync on. Put eggs in your incubators before you start, close the game, keep the phone in a pocket or running belt, and walk. The distance has to appear in Apple Health or Google Fit first, and the game can take up to several hours to sync it. With Adventure Sync off, a treadmill walk will not hatch eggs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a speed limit for Adventure Sync?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The official Pokémon GO help pages we checked in October 2026 do not publish one. Fan sites quote various thresholds, but they disagree, so we do not repeat them. The help pages describe Adventure Sync in terms of walking distance. If you run on the treadmill, compare your health app's distance with what Adventure Sync credits afterwards to see how your set-up behaves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I enter my treadmill distance manually for Pokémon GO?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The Pokémon GO Help Center says fitness data entered manually into Apple Health or Google Fit does not count toward Adventure Sync. Only distance your phone's health app records itself is credited. If your treadmill walks are not counting, check phone placement, location permission and battery-saving settings instead of typing the console distance in.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will spoofing my location get me banned?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can. The game's Gameplay Fairness Policy names GPS location spoofing as cheating under a three-strike policy: a warning, a suspension of roughly 30 days, then a permanent ban. It also says some behaviour can lead to immediate termination without warning, and that very few terminations are overturned on appeal. Adventure Sync is the legitimate way to count treadmill walking.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Playing Pokémon GO on a Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">When treadmill walking does not count, it is almost always one of these four.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the phone in the console tray</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A phone counts steps from its own movement, and the console is where it moves least. The belt passes under you while the phone records almost nothing, so Apple Health or Google Fit has no walk for Adventure Sync to read. Put the phone in a snug trouser pocket or a running belt instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping the game open instead of using Adventure Sync</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">With Adventure Sync off, the open game relies on your movement on its map, and on a treadmill you never move on that map, however far you walk. Switch Adventure Sync on in Settings, General, allow access to your health app, and set location permission to always allow. Then close the game, pocket the phone and walk.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Typing the console distance into the health app</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The help centre says manually entered fitness data does not count toward Adventure Sync. Adding a treadmill walk by hand to Apple Health or Google Fit, however accurate the console figure, will not hatch an egg. Fix the source instead: phone placement, permissions and battery-saving settings, then give the sync a few hours.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reaching for a spoofing app</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Location spoofing is named as cheating in the game's Gameplay Fairness Policy, with penalties that run from a warning to a permanent ban, sometimes without any warning at all. It is a poor trade for a walk you were going to take anyway. Adventure Sync is the legitimate way to get credit indoors.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

